# SYNOPSIS

```usage
vx build [arch ...]
vx test [arch ...] [--only test,...]
vx test --changed [ref] [--plan]
vx test --check
vx boot [arch] [--cpu [key] | --desktop]
```

# DESCRIPTION

These commands drive CMake's per-arch trees, `build/<arch>/`, which the presets in `CMakePresets.json` configure.
An <arch> is `amd64`, `arm64` or `rv64`.

`vx build` configures each <arch> given, once, and builds it.
With none it builds all three.
A tree is configured when it has no `build.ninja`; after that only the build runs.

`vx test` builds each <arch>, then runs that arch's QEMU tests with `ctest`, one arch after another, each arch's boots several at once: one for every four of the host's CPUs (each QEMU has four vCPUs), at least one.
No arch's run should take more than 5 minutes; a check that flakes under this load is removed, not retried.
With no <arch> it tests all three.
It stops at the first arch whose build or tests fail, and exits 1.
`--only` runs just the named `ctest` tests of each <arch>, after its build; a name an arch doesn't have is reported and skipped.

`vx boot` builds one <arch> (`amd64` by default) and boots it interactively in QEMU, with edk2 firmware from `/usr/share/edk2`, by running `ninja -C build/<arch> run`.
Ctrl-A x quits QEMU.
More than one <arch> is refused.
With `--cpu` it boots the cpu image instead: the launcher and every service, with a cpu server (cpu(8)) for the terminal whose public key is <key>, 64 hex digits, or this terminal's own (vx-cpu(1)) without one.
The key goes to `build/<arch>/cpu/terminal`, which the image carries as `lib/cpu/terminal`, and vx's node key (vx-cpu(1)) to `build/<arch>/cpu/keys`, which it carries as factotum's `lib/factotum/keys`; the node's port 17019 is forwarded from 17019 on 127.0.0.1, so `vx cpu localhost` reaches it.
No other image has a cpu server.
Where tier 1 is built, the cpu image also has the peer comparison's program, `bin/peer-bench` (`tools/peer-bench`), run from a cpu session.
With `--desktop` it boots the desktop's image: the launcher and every service, with radharc(4) on the framebuffer, which takes the keyboard from the console's shell.

`vx test --changed` runs only what a change puts at risk.
The change is every file that differs from where the tree left <ref> (`main` by default): committed, staged, unstaged and untracked.
Each file's row in `docs/risk.tsv` names what it risks; the longest path matching the file wins, and a file with no row risks everything.
A build file (`CMakeLists.txt`, `kernel/CMakeLists.txt`, `cmake/CroiUser.cmake`, `cmake/Tier1.cmake`) is read for what its changed lines name: the sources under `${PROJECT_SOURCE_DIR}/` or `${CMAKE_SOURCE_DIR}/`, directly or through one of the file's own `_name` variables set to them, stand in for it, and the tests named (`boot-...`, `...-pixels`) run on each tested arch.
A changed line that names neither, the build's own machinery, also runs the file's row, a small canary set; a global such as `CROI_QEMU` isn't followed.
The plan is the union of the rows: the SwiftPM test targets, run by one `swift test --filter`; each arch to build, with its `ctest` tests or its whole suite; and the checks among `vx man --check`, `vx loc --check`, `vx hardware --check`, `vx rc-oracle` and `vx test --check`.
`vx` prints the plan, then runs it, and exits 1 if any part fails.
An arch whose suite is off by default is built, not tested.
`--plan` prints the plan and runs nothing.
This is the test run for work in progress; the full suite, `vx ci`, is the one that judges a milestone.

`vx test --check` checks `docs/risk.tsv` against the tree: every path in it exists, every test target is in `Package.swift`, every test name is one of its arches' `ctest` tests, and every test target and every test of `amd64` and `arm64` is named by some row (a row of `all` names nothing).
An arch whose tree isn't configured has its names left unchecked, and says so.
`vx ci` runs it.

Each step's output goes to `build/vx/<arch>-configure.log`, `<arch>-build.log` or `<arch>-test.log`.
`vx boot`'s own run is not logged: it owns the terminal.

## risk.tsv

A tab-separated file; `#` starts a comment line, and the first other line names the columns: `path`, `arches`, `host`, `boots`, `checks`.
A <path> ending in `/` is a directory, and matches every file under it; any other is one file.
<arches> is a comma list of arches, or `all` for `amd64` and `arm64`; <host>, SwiftPM test targets, or `all` for every one; <boots>, `ctest` names, or `all` for each arch's whole suite; <checks>, names among `man`, `loc`, `hardware`, `rc-oracle` and `risk`.
`-` is none.

# FILES

: `build/<arch>/`
  The CMake tree.
: `build/vx/*.log`
  Each step's output.
: `CMakePresets.json`
  The presets, one per arch.
: `docs/risk.tsv`
  What a change under each path risks.

